 could not invert the full 64-pixel system by hand, so I used a shared low-dimensional image description that both networks can be projected onto. I split the useful pixels (columns 1–6) into 6 regions and treated ink as constant within each region:\n- top (rows 0–1)\n- upper-left and upper-right (rows 2–3)\n- lower-left and lower-right (rows 4–5)\n- bottom (rows 6–7)\n\nThe construction had five steps:\n1. **A's view of the regions.** For each region r, I summed A's first-layer weight rows over its pixels to get G_r (a 16-vector). If the region means are f, then h1A − bA ≈ f·G, ignoring the ReLU.\n2. **Recovering the region means.** I solved for f by least squares with a small ridge term: f̂ = (h1A − bA)·Gᵀ(GGᵀ + 3I)⁻¹. I factored the 6×6 Gram matrix by LDLᵀ and solved it for every A unit, which gives U (16×6). A check showed that recovering the top region alone gives about 0.94, close to 1.\n3. **B's view of the regions.** I summed B's first-layer weight rows over the same regions to get V (6×24). B's pre-activation for a block image is then f·V + bB.\n4. **The bridge.** M = U·V, with row 7 set to zero because that unit is dead.\n5. **The offset.** c = bB − bA·M, so that A's bias is cancelled before the region means are mapped into B.\n\nThe result is a blurred, region-level copy of the image passed into B. It is approximate: the ReLU clipping in A and the variation of ink within each region are ignored.\n\n**Bridge:**\n```json\n{\"M\": [\n[0.064, 0.082, -0.190, 0.075, 0.083, 0.185, 0.080, 0.041, 0.087, -0.107, -0.059, 0.036, -0.135, 0.073, 0.003, 0.002, 0.118, 0.155, -0.058, 0.093, 0.131, -0.243, -0.027, -0.094],\n[0.222, 0.141, -0.109, -0.060, 0.056, -0.077, 0.228, 0.107, 0.073, 0.041, -0.039, 0.161, 0.056, -0.061, 0.169, -0.051, 0.129, -0.051, -0.030, 0.018, 0.061, 0.018, 0.052, 0.001],\n[0.252, 0.240, 0.035, 0.075, 0.009, 0.048, 0.256, 0.174, 0.077, 0.106, 0.028, 0.012, -0.045, 0.099, 0.065, 0.071, 0.145, -0.057, -0.006, 0.182, -0.090, -0.075, -0.050, -0.063],